 @john_hind  that's definitely part of the equation, but consider that as you go up in voltage you need a diode with higher reverse breakdown, and those also have higher forward voltage. So between like 0V and 50V it's not very linear, but above that the forward voltage goes up with reverse breakdown, and eventually you're having to just stack diodes in series, and at that point it is definitely linear again.

Besides that, as current increases, so does heat dissipation, so (as hinted earlier in this thread) the decision to use something like this may be less about the percentage efficiency and more about whether you can avoid putting in a heat sink and fan. The heat generation is going to relate directly to the current and not at all the voltage.

You're right to be a little confused because it's not explained very clearly, but also, the simple models we're taught are a little too simple as well. I think the truth presented here is at least as accurate.

 @EternityForest  Efficiency and power factor laws? Where? I've never seen such a thing here in the US. 

Even if those laws exist or come into existence, this doesn't mean some Chinese company won't produce a cheaper version of your product that used a single diode and does the same job for half the price, which some people will definitely end up buying. 

Not every device is, can be, or should be powered via USB, and devices which do not support the Power Delivery standards won't just disappear overnight either. If it gets widely adopted it will still take a long time for everything else to get mostly phased out anyways, in the mean time the single diode will continue to live.

